# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
# .env.local is git-ignored — never commit real secrets.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTE — two different files:
#   .env.local  → secrets below, loaded INTO the backend container (env_file)
#   .env        → deployment settings, read BY Docker Compose for ${...} in
#                 docker-compose.yml. Both are git-ignored.
#
# Local dev needs no .env at all — the defaults cover it. On the deployed VM
# (behind Cloudflare + the init.d gateway, which forwards to the VM's port 4173)
# create a root .env like:
#
#   PUBLIC_URL=https://assignment-hub.cskmitl.com
#   BIND=127.0.0.1
#   FRONTEND_PORT=4174
#
# then bring the stack up with the proxy profile:
#
#   docker compose --profile tls up -d
#
#   PUBLIC_URL     origin the browser uses. Builds the OAuth redirect URIs, so
#                  it must match what's registered with Google/Azure exactly.
#                  Without it login bounces to http://localhost:4173.
#   BIND           interface the app ports publish on. 127.0.0.1 keeps the
#                  frontend/backend/db ports private — MySQL's root password is
#                  in this repo, so never leave this at 0.0.0.0 on a server.
#   FRONTEND_PORT  host port for the frontend container. Must not be 4173 on
#                  the VM, because Caddy takes 4173 for the gateway.
#   PROXY_PORT     (optional) port Caddy publishes for the gateway; 4173 by
#                  default. Change it only if the gateway's target port changes.
#
# Also add the hostname to `server.allowedHosts` in frontend/vite.config.js, or
# Vite rejects the request with a 403.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- Google OAuth ---
# Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials → OAuth client ID (Web application)
# Authorized redirect URI: http://localhost:4173/api/auth/google/callback
# One callback is all that is needed — administrators sign in through the same
# flow and get their access from a User_Role grant, not a second login.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Microsoft OAuth ---
# Azure Portal → Azure Active Directory → App registrations → New registration
# Redirect URI (Web): http://localhost:4173/api/auth/microsoft/callback
# MS_CLIENT_ID    = "Application (client) ID" on the app's Overview page
# MS_CLIENT_SECRET = the secret's "Value" from Certificates & secrets (not the Secret ID)
# MS_TENANT_ID    = "Directory (tenant) ID" on the app's Overview page
#   (only needed if the app is single-tenant; omit to default to "organizations")
MS_CLIENT_ID=
MS_CLIENT_SECRET=
MS_TENANT_ID=

# --- Email (SMTP) ---
# Used by the reminder sender (FR-07). Leave these empty and the backend still
# runs — reminder emails are printed to `docker compose logs backend` instead of
# being sent, which is enough to exercise the whole pipeline in dev.
#
# Gmail: you need an App Password, not your account password. Enable 2-Step
# Verification, then Google Account → Security → App passwords.
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
#   SMTP_PORT=587
#   SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
#   SMTP_PASS=<16-character app password, no spaces>
#
# Mailtrap (safest for testing — nothing leaves the sandbox):
#   SMTP_HOST=sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io
#   SMTP_PORT=2525
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
# Sender shown to the recipient. Gmail rewrites this to the authenticated
# account, so it only matters on providers that allow a custom from address.
MAIL_FROM=
# Set to 1 only for implicit TLS on a port other than 465 (465 turns it on
# automatically; 587 uses STARTTLS and needs nothing here).
# SMTP_SECURE=

# --- Session ---
# Any long random string, e.g. generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
SESSION_SECRET=
